// Pathfinder Run custom pedestrian model.
//
// Implements the static path-type weighting from
// docs/running-app-architecture.md §5, point 1's first bullet: prefer
// dedicated pedestrian infrastructure, penalize (not exclude) unprotected
// major roads, exclude anything without foot access.
//
// Deliberately NOT included here (separate, later work):
// - Edge-reuse penalty (app-level logic in the route generator, not a
//   GraphHopper profile concern)
// - The live closures penalty (per-request only -- see generate_loop.py's
//   fetch_return_leg/fetch_outbound_leg -- because it changes request to
//   request)
//
// Greenness/park-proximity (§5 point 1's second bullet): RESTORED, via a
// static "greenspace" encoded value baked in at import time -- not the
// old `in_greenspace` custom_areas mechanism (removed during the
// full-Ontario migration; see git history around that removal for why:
// a live per-edge point-in-polygon lookup against 315,762 polygons was
// the confirmed root cause of /route being unusably slow in flexible
// mode at province scale, disambiguated directly -- 49ms vs 22-56s for
// an identical query with only that rule stripped out).
//
// This time it costs nothing per query, the same way road_class/surface/
// etc. already don't: graphhopper-ext/ (a small standalone Java tool, run
// once at import time, not part of the web server) registers "greenspace"
// as a real encoded value via GraphHopper's ImportRegistry extension
// point, sourced from scripts/compute_greenspace_way_ids.py's offline
// precomputation (an OSM-way-id set, not live polygon geometry --
// GraphHopper's TagParser callback only exposes tags/node-ids during
// import, not resolved coordinates, so the actual point-in-polygon spatial
// join happens once, offline, in Python with a proper spatial index,
// not per-way in Java).
//
// Confirmed empirically, not assumed, that this is genuinely free at
// serve time: GraphHopper.load() (all the production server ever does)
// reconstructs its EncodingManager via EncodingManager.fromProperties(...),
// confirmed via decompilation to be completely independent of the
// ImportRegistry -- so the stock, unmodified graphhopper-web.jar serves a
// graph built by graphhopper-ext/'s importer with zero server-side code
// changes, and CH speed mode stays fully engaged (verified:
// dijkstrabi|ch-routing in the log, not a flexible-mode fallback). See
// graphhopper-ext/README.md for the full mechanism and how to rebuild.
//
// Note: this replaces the stock model's `multiply_by: foot_priority` rather than
// layering on top of it. GraphHopper's built-in `foot_priority` (FootPriorityParser)
// already does its own road-class weighting AND reads the literal `sidewalk=*` tag
// on a way directly -- a signal not exposed as any encoded value queryable here, so
// this explicit model can't distinguish "primary road with a mapped sidewalk" from
// "primary road with none." Keeping both would double-penalize/boost the same
// categories unpredictably, so this is an explicit tradeoff: visible, tunable rules
// here, at the cost of that sidewalk-tag signal until it's reintroduced deliberately.
//
// TRUNK/MOTORWAY get their own (lower) tier rather than sharing PRIMARY/SECONDARY's:
// the stock model penalizes fast/limited-access roads far harder than ordinary
// arterials (REACH_DESTINATION = 0.1x vs BAD = 0.5x for primary/secondary), and this
// keeps that same margin rather than flattening it.
{
  "priority": [
    { "if": "!foot_access", "multiply_by": "0" },
    { "if": "road_class == PATH || road_class == FOOTWAY || road_class == PEDESTRIAN || road_class == LIVING_STREET", "multiply_by": "1.5" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == PRIMARY || road_class == SECONDARY", "multiply_by": "0.3" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == TRUNK || road_class == MOTORWAY", "multiply_by": "0.1" },
    { "if": "greenspace", "multiply_by": "1.3" }
  ],
  "speed": [
    { "if": "true", "limit_to": "foot_average_speed" }
  ]
}
